CORE
main.cpp
--cpp20 --unwind 4 --no-unwinding-assertions --object-bits 10
^\[main\.assertion\.1\] line \d+ member-template ctor reconstructing the class pack has its body instantiated: SUCCESS$
^VERIFICATION SUCCESSFUL$
^EXIT=0$
^SIGNAL=0$
--
^.*no body for callee.*$
^CONVERSION ERROR$
--
[temp.variadic] + [temp.mem]: a member function template of a class template
partial-specialized on a function type R(Args...) whose body uses the enclosing
class's parameter pack Args... to form a template argument (reconstructing
R(Args...) to instantiate another template) now has its body instantiated.  This
is the std::function converting-constructor pattern
  typedef _Function_handler<_Res(_ArgTypes...), _Functor> _Handler;
  _M_invoker = &_Handler::_M_invoke;

Root cause: template_mapt::build assumed a parameter pack was always the last
template parameter (`template_parameters().back()`), so for a class template
partial specialization whose pack is followed by another parameter -- e.g.
`template <class R, class... A, class F> struct H<R(A...), F>`, the shape of
_Function_handler -- the pack was never recorded in pack_args_map/pack_size_map.
The member's body that expands the pack then failed to elaborate and was left
empty (so _M_invoker was never assigned and std::function calls dereferenced a
null function pointer).  Fixed by detecting and binding a parameter pack at any
position in build() and aligning the parameters after the pack to the trailing
arguments.  (A companion parser fix -- cpp11_function_param_pack_not_varargs --
stops a trailing `Args...` from being parsed as a C-style variadic ellipsis.)
